{
  "result": "At 4 a.m., the world belongs to no one. The streets are empty, the sky is a deep, bruised blue, and even the air feels different \u2014 thinner, cleaner, as if the planet itself is taking a breath before the day begins. I love that hour not because it is productive or peaceful in any Instagram-meditation way, but because it is honest.\n\nDuring the day, everything is performing. Buildings try to look important, people try to look busy, even trees seem to be posing for an invisible audience. But at 4 a.m., the performance stops. A streetlight buzzes without anyone to illuminate. A cat crosses the road without hurrying. Your own thoughts, stripped of the day's noise, finally speak in their normal voices.\n\nI think we spend most of our lives waiting for big moments \u2014 graduations, promotions, loves, losses \u2014 and we miss that life is actually made of these small, unobserved interludes. The way light falls across a kitchen counter. The way a stranger holds the door a second too long just to be kind. The way old paper smells. \n\nThere is no grand lesson in these things. They don't make you richer or more successful. But they are, I think, the only things that ever make you feel truly awake. If you can notice them, you haven't missed your life while waiting for it to start. You are already inside it. And perhaps that quiet noticing is the closest we ever get to prayer without saying anything.",
